# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env and pick ONE database option below.
# .env is gitignored — never commit it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── OPTION 1: Local dev (zero setup) ─────────────────────
# Uses a bundled SQLite file. Run `npm run setup` once to
# create the database and load 116 sample employees.
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"

# ── OPTION 2: Production / shared dev (Postgres) ─────────
# Sign up free at https://neon.tech, create a project, copy
# the connection string here. Then run `npm run setup`.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secret for signing JWT session cookies.
# Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JWT_SECRET="meridian-hr-dev-secret-change-in-production-9f8a7b6c5d4e3f2a1b0c9d8e7f6a5b4c"



# How it works now
# For anyone running locally (5 commands, zero setup)

# git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/meridian-hr.git
# cd meridian-hr
# cp .env.example .env
# npm install
# npm run setup
# npm run dev
# That's it. The default .env points to SQLite, npm run setup creates dev.db and seeds 116 employees, then npm run dev opens at http://localhost:5173. No database server to install, no signups.

# For deploying to Vercel (one-time setup)
# DEPLOY.md walks through it. Summary:

# Push code to GitHub
# Create free Neon Postgres
# From your laptop, seed Neon: node scripts/prepare-build.mjs --postgres && DATABASE_URL="..." npm run setup then node scripts/prepare-build.mjs --sqlite to restore your local schema
# Import to Vercel + paste env vars → click Deploy
# Vercel auto-swaps to Postgres at build time. You never edit schema.prisma manually.

# The magic: scripts/prepare-build.mjs
# A tiny Node script that toggles just the provider = "..." line in prisma/schema.prisma:

# Trigger	What it does
# node scripts/prepare-build.mjs (no flag)	Does nothing on local — leaves SQLite alone
# Same, but Vercel set VERCEL=1	Auto-switches to postgresql
# --postgres flag	Manually switches to Postgres
# --sqlite flag	Manually restores SQLite
# Single source of truth — only one schema.prisma file. Models stay in sync forever.

# What changed
# File	Change
# prisma/schema.prisma	Header comment explains the dual-mode flow; provider = "sqlite" is the committed default
# scripts/prepare-build.mjs	NEW — toggles the provider line in-place
# package.json	build now runs prepare-build.mjs first; new npm run setup alias for first-time DB creation
# vercel.json	Removed custom buildCommand so Vercel uses the updated npm run build
# .env.example	Shows both SQLite (default, uncommented) and Postgres (commented) options
# .env	Already on file:./dev.db — local dev ready
# README.md	"Run locally" section is now 5 lines; mentions DEPLOY.md for production
# DEPLOY.md	Rewritten — no manual schema editing; explains the dual-mode mechanism
# START_HERE.txt	Updated with the new commands
# Verified
# ✅ All 4 prepare-build cases (no flag / --postgres / --sqlite / VERCEL=1) toggle correctly
# ✅ TypeScript: zero errors
# ✅ All 10 pages render HTTP 200 on local SQLite
# ✅ Schema returns to SQLite after each test
# ✅ Prisma client regenerates cleanly
